{"name":"Cardamom Spice","hex":"#837165","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cardamom","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An herb, native to India (Elettaria cardamomum).","Seed of Elettaria cardamomum, used as a medicine and spice, especially in curry powder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cardamom"},{"term":"spice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Aromatic or pungent plant matter (usually dried) used to season or flavor food.","The quality of being spicy."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add spice or spices to; season.","To spice up."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spice"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cardamom","blocks":[{"text":"Cardamom, sometimes cardamon or cardamum, is a spice made from the seeds of several plants in the genera Elettaria and Amomum in the family Zingiberaceae. Both genera are native to the Indian subcontinent and Indonesia. They are recognized by their small seed pods: triangular in cross-section and spindle-shaped, with a thin, papery outer shell and small, black seeds; Elettaria pods are light green and smaller, while Amomum pods are dark brown and larger."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/02017_0119_Kardamom%2C_Winter_in_den_Beskiden.jpg/330px-02017_0119_Kardamom%2C_Winter_in_den_Beskiden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cardamom","wikidata":"Q14625808"},{"title":"Spice","blocks":[{"text":"In the culinary arts, a spice is a seed, fruit, root, bark, or other plant substance primarily used for flavouring or colouring food. Spices are distinguished from herbs, which are the leaves, flowers, or stems of plants used for flavouring or as a garnish. Spices and herbs are both seasonings. \nSpices are sometimes used in medicine, religious rituals, cosmetics, or perfume production. They are usually classified into spices, spice seeds, and herbal categories. For example, vanilla is commonly used as an ingredient in fragrance manufacturing. Plant-based sweeteners such as sugar are not considered spices."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Spices1.jpg/330px-Spices1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spice","wikidata":"Q42527"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Elettaria_cardamomum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-057.jpg/330px-Elettaria_cardamomum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-057.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Elettaria_cardamomum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-057.jpg/330px-Elettaria_cardamomum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-057.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"True cardamom plant (Elettaria cardamomum)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElettaria_cardamomum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-057.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Cardamom_Seeds_BNC.jpg/500px-Cardamom_Seeds_BNC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Cardamom_Seeds_BNC.jpg/1280px-Cardamom_Seeds_BNC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Cardamom_Seeds_BNC.jpg/500px-Cardamom_Seeds_BNC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cardamom seeds","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACardamom_Seeds_BNC.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Cardamom_%28Kerala%2C_India%29.jpg/500px-Cardamom_%28Kerala%2C_India%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=th